107-                  Methods
108-                  Soil and feedstock sampling and preparation
109-                  Soils of two different texture classes i.e. silty loam (PK) and sandy loam (BA), were col-
110-                  lected for greenhouse experiments from two sites i.e. a wastewater irrigated urban veg-
111:                  etable farming site in Addis Ababa and a rainfed peri-urban groundnut farming site in
112-                  Babile, Ethiopia. At each site, approximately 100 kg of composite soil sample was exca-
113-                  vated from the surface to a depth of 15 cm. The soil samples were transported to the
114-                  greenhouse in plastic bags. The samples were air-dried, homogenized, and sieved using
115-                  a <2 mm sieve.
116-
117-                  Stabilization treatments
118-                  Faecal matter (FM) was collected from septage drying bed in Addis Ababa sewage treat-
119-                  ment plant. Samples were taken from 12 different locations at 10 cm depth, then mixed
120-                  into one composite sample. Poultry litter (PL) was also obtained from drying bed in a
121-                  commercial deep—bedded poultry farm in Bishoftu. Cow manure (CM) was collected
122-                  from a private milking facility. Prosopis juliflora (PJ) pods were collected from different
123-                  Prosopis juliflora invaded lands in a peri-urban area of Dire Dawa. Coffee husk (CH) was
