168-                  Analyses
169-                  First, the soil and biochar samples were ground to <2 mm. For total element, NH4NO3
170:                  extractable trace elements and Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) analyses, soils and
171-                  biochar samples were milled with a planetary ball mill to achieve a homogeneous fine
172-                  powder (Fritsch GmbH, Idar-Oberstein, Germany). The pH of biochar in water was
173-                  determined in 1:20 (w/v) ratio after occasionally stirring over 1 h (Cheng et al. 2006). The
174-                  pH of the soils in water suspensions were determined in 1:2.5 (w/v) ratio after shaking
175-                  over 2 h. The EC of biochar was determined after 1 h equilibration of 1 g of biochar with
176-                  20 ml of distilled water. The EC of the soil was determined after 2 h equilibration of 1 g
177-                  of soil with 2.5 ml of distilled water. Soil Particle size distributions were determined by
178-                  laser diffraction using an Analysette 22 MicroTec plus (Fritsch GmbH, Idar-Oberstein,
179-                  Germany) with a wet dispersion unit. For total element analysis, 0.25 g of biochar and
180-                  soil were placed into 50 ml vessels, followed by addition of 10 ml concentrated HNO3.
181-                  The mixtures were left to cold digest in a fume cupboard over night and then heated in
182-                  1.6 KW microwave oven for 30 min. After cooling to room temperature, 10 ml of dou-
