239-                  Results and discussion
240-                  Characterization of soils and stabilization treatments
241:                  Table 1 shows selected properties of PK and BA soils. PK soil was silty loam having
242-                  pH 6.71(H2O) and relatively high in exchangeable cations compared to BA soil. The
243-                  pH(H2O) of BA soil was 6.86 with a sandy loam texture. The total Cd concentration of
244-                  PK soil (2.58 mg/kg) was higher than BA soil (0.30 mg/kg). Soil carbon status of PK soil
245-                  was rated as moderate, whereas soil carbon concentration of BA soil was rated as very
246-                  low according to Tekalign (1991). Similarly, BA soil had a low total N content as com-
247-                  pared to the critical concentration reported in Peverill et al. (1999).
248-                    In contrast to the more alkaline pH(H2O) of CM, PJ and PL biochars, biochars from
249:                  CH and FM had slightly alkaline pH values (Additional file 1: Table S1). ES also had high
250-                  pH value of 9.28 and contained considerable amount of calcite (CaCO3) (Lee et al. 2013).
251-                  Similarly, biochars produced from PJ, PL and CM had high EC values, whereas, CH and
252-                  FC biochars exhibited low EC values. These were expected considering the high salt/
253-                  ash content in CM and PL biochars (Cantrell et al. 2012). The biochar treatments had
254-                  varied total C concentration, with FM < CM < PL < PJ < CH. Unlike CH biochar which
