278-                  exhibited the highest concentration of total C and the lowest concentration of total N
279-                  typical feature of plant-based biochars (Gaskin et al. 2008; Singh et al. 2010), the other
280-                  biochar treatments including PJ had very high concentrations of total N (Additional
281:                  file 1: Table S1). Moreover, CH biochar had the highest surface area (206 m2/g). The total
282-                  surface acidity of the examined biochar treatments ranged from 0.42 to 3.24 mmol/g
283:                  (Additional file 1: Table S1). The acidic surface functionality might caused by the pres-
284-                  ence of carboxyl, phenolic and lactonic groups. Whereas, ketones, carbonates and other
285-                  alkaline species might be responsible for basic surface functionality (Mukerjee et al.
286-                  2011). With the exception of CH biochar, total acidic surface functionalities of the bio-
287-                  char treatments were less than their corresponding basic functionalities. These obser-
288-                  vations were consistent with the study of Singh et al. (2010), who recorded high total
289-                  surface basicity than surface acidity in PL and CM biochars produced at 550 °C with
290-                  steam activation and Uras et al. (2012) who reported high surface acidity than surface
291-                  basicity in plant based biochars.
292-                     Although faecal matter and manure derived biochar treatments had high concen-
293-                  trations of total P and major cations, the total P, Fe, Al and Mg concentrations in FM
294-                  biochar were higher than the concentrations in other biochar treatments (Additional
295:                  file 1: Table S2). Yet again, the FM biochar had the highest total trace elements. How-
296-                  ever, CM and PJ biochars contained the highest concentrations of Ca (34 g kg−1) and
297-                  K (39.2 g kg−1), respectively. The high levels of P, K, Mg and Ca in the biochars were
298-                  consistent with the results of Song and Guo (2012), who reported very high concentra-
299-                  tions of these elements in PL biochars produced under various pyrolysis temperatures.
300-                  The highest exchangeable K (59.6 cmol(+) kg−1) was observed in PJ biochar, while the
301-                  lowest (1.60 and 1.61 cmol(+) kg−1) were recorded in CH and FM biochars, respectively.
302-                  However, CM and PL biochars exhibited the highest exchangeable Mg and Ca concen-
303-                  trations, respectively. Generally, the CEC of the biochar treatments were in the order
304:                  of PJ > CM > PL > FM > CH (Additional file 1: Table S3). In comparison, the CEC of
305-                  PL biochar was 12.2 % higher than similar biochar with an average value of 37 cmol(+)
306-                  kg−1 despite the fact that the methods of CEC measurement differed (Song and Guo
307-                  2012). There were also differences in Olsen-P (available P) concentrations of biochar
308:                  treatments, with CMB > FMB > PLB > PJ > CH (Additional file 1: Table S3). As expected
309-                  plant-based biochar treatments exhibited the lowest Olsen-P values of 28.1 mg kg−1
310-                  (CH) and 383 mg kg−1 (PJ). On the contrary to the total P, the highest available P
311-                  (1437 mg kg−1) was exhibited by CM biochar. Likewise, the study of Cao and Harris
312-                  (2010) showed very high water soluble P value of CM biochar produced under very low
313-                  pyrolysis temperature. Available P value of FM biochar decreased to higher degree to its
314:                  corresponding total P value, this was largely ascribed to the formation of stable P con-
315-                  taining compounds.
316-                     Biosolids are known to contain high total concentrations of trace and toxic elements,
317-                  which exist in more pronounced concentrations in charred product (Bridle and Pritchard
318-                  2004; Lu et al. 2013). However, the use of biochar from biosolid is highly limited by the
319-                  bioavailability nature of the trace and toxic elements than the total load. Ammonium
320:                  nitrate extractable fraction was used to estimate the bioavailability of heavy metals in the
321-                  examined biochar treatments. The mobile fractions of the metals in the biochar treat-
322-                  ments accounted very small portion of their corresponding total contents. For example,
