329-                  and 0.04 % of the total loads of Cd, Co, Cr, Cu, Ni, Pb and Zn, respectively (Additional
330:                  file 1: Tables S2, S3). Overall, the bioavailable fractions in the biochars were in the range
331-                  of 0.47–2.5, 0.14–0.85, 0.02–0.09, 0.015–0.11, 0.04–0.71, 0.005–0.76 and 0.04–7.76 % of
332:                  the total loads of Cd, Co, Cr, Cu, Ni, Pb and Zn, respectively (Additional file 1: Tables
333-                  S2, S3). FTIR spectra of FM and PL biochars were very similar (Additional file 1: Fig-
334-                  ure S1). The characteristics broad bands at 3419, 3442,3419, 3431 and 3466 cm−1 were
335-                  attributed to the stretching vibrations of hydrogen-bonded hydroxyl groups of FM, PL,
336-                  CM, PJ and CH biochars, respectively (Keiluweit et al. 2010). For all biochars, but CHB,
337-                  aromatic C=C ring stretching were observed between 1462 and 1433 cm−1.The presence
338-                  of C=O stretching vibrations (1700–1600) indicated the presence of carboxylic groups
339-                  and ketones. Considering the high P contents of faecal and manure derived biochars,
340-                  particularly FMB and CMB, the intense broad bands at 1038 cm−1 likely resulted from
341-                  P-containing functional groups, most importantly, P-O bond of phosphate functional
342-                  group (Jiang et al. 2004).
